<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Sailendra Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">752 CE – 832 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Buddhist dynasty of Central Java that built Borobudur, the largest Buddhist monument in the world, in the late 8th and early 9th centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sailendra-dynasty">Sailendras</a>, 'lords of the mountain', appear in inscriptions from the 770s. The Kalasan inscription of 778 records a temple to the goddess Tara built under their patronage. Whether they were a separate kingdom or a branch ruling within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/medang-kingdom">Medang Kingdom</a> is still debated, as is their possible origin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> or mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The dynasty were patrons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana Buddhism</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaratungga">Samaratungga</a> the great stupa-mountain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borobudur">Borobudur</a> was completed around 825, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mendut">Mendut</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pawon">Pawon</a>. They kept close ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>, and their power was felt as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a> and possibly <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-cambodia-1953-1970">Cambodia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaratungga">Samaratungga's</a> daughter Pramodhawardhani married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rakai-pikatan">Rakai Pikatan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanjaya-of-mataram">Sanjaya</a> line, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sailendra-dynasty">Sailendras</a> lost their hold on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a> in the mid-9th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balaputradewa">Balaputradewa</a>, a Sailendra prince whose exact relationship to her is debated, went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> and became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>, where the Sailendra name continued for centuries.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sailendra-kingdom.jpg" alt="Sailendra Kingdom" width="320" height="164"><figcaption>Image: Gunawan Kartapranata · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sailendra_King_and_Queen,_Borobudur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>752 CE – 832 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Samaratungga, Pramodhawardhani, Balaputradewa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mahayana Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Javanese, Old Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sailendra dynasty, Shailendra</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=792&amp;focus=sailendra-kingdom" aria-label="Sailendra Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7525 2219 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sailendra Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7833 2434h0M8208 2754h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7845 2475h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7845 2475h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Indonesia. <a href="/atlas?year=792&amp;focus=sailendra-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
